    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.1038/s43247-026-03208-5",
    "abstract": "Over 100 countries have announced net-zero emissions goals. Although these net-zero emissions goals may be consistent with global 2 \u00b0C climate stabilization, their impacts on equity between developing and developed regions remain poorly understood. Here, we used an integrated assessment model and developed scenarios based on the National Determined Contributions and Net Zero Emissions targets, aiming to achieve the 2 \u00b0C goals, along with a baseline scenario that followed the Shared Socioeconomic Pathway 2 baseline projections. Moreover, we show the extent to which individual national net-zero emissions goals are far from equitable and explored potential measures. The results indicate that net-zero emissions without financial support causes large macroeconomic losses in developing countries. The financial transfer of approximately 2.7 trillion US dollars per year to developing regions equivalent to 5% of household consumption in developed regions is sufficient to alleviate such economic burdens. If developed countries should undertake additional physical emissions reductions, the required carbon dioxide removal will scale to 26 Gigatons of carbon dioxide per year, equivalent to over 10% of household consumption. This result suggests that rather than pursuing additional net-negative emissions in developed countries, international financial transfer could be a more pragmatic approach to attain the global net-zero emissions goal. Current national net-zero pledges risk large economic losses in developing regions, and international financial transfers of about 2.7 trillion US dollars per year could effectively offset these impacts, according to a computable general equilibrium model and scenario analysis.",
    "field": "Climate change economics",
    "problem": "The equity impacts of current national net-zero emissions goals are poorly understood, creating a trade-off where developing nations face disproportionately large economic burdens without international financial support to alleviate these macroeconomic losses.",
    "method": "This study uses an integrated assessment model to develop and compare scenarios exploring how international financial transfers or additional emissions reductions by developed countries can alleviate the macroeconomic burden on developing nations that results from pursuing net-zero emissions goals, thereby resolving the inherent equity trade-off.",
    "task": "The task is to assess the equity trade-offs of national net-zero emissions goals between developed and developing countries and to evaluate potential solutions, such as international financial transfers or additional emissions reductions, using an integrated assessment model.",
